class Solution {
  public int prefixStencilsFloorTicket(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[]sums=new int[a.length+1];for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++)sums[i+1]=sums[i]+a[i];for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++)out.add(sums[i+1]-sums[Math.max(0,i-k+1)]);}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.min(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

For each position in readings, add the current number and up to k - 1 numbers just before it. Stop at the start of the input if needed. Record each sum, from left to right. For readings = [2, 3, 4] and k = 2, the result list is [2, 5, 7].

Return the answer

Return the smallest number in the result list. If the list is empty, return 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

